{
  "abstract": "We prove that a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(98,20,9,8,3)$ must have four distinct closed in-neighborhoods whose indicators have even sum at every vertex, and four closed out-neighborhoods with the same property. In at least one orientation the indicators form an integer parallelogram, so the sum of two neighborhoods equals the sum of the other two. The proof starts from a shortest odd cover by eight neighborhoods. If no four distinct indicators had even sum, the remaining types would occupy distinct points of a four-dimensional binary quotient. Two independent enumerations leave 211 configurations. Integer certificates exclude their common vertex populations using the singleton cells of every shortest cover, the degree constraints and the intersections of distinct neighborhoods. We then classify the real geometry of four indicators of even sum. At real rank four they generate six disjoint mutual cliques of size seven. Any mutual clique of size at least seven meeting their union is one of the six. The transposed range identity prevents this configuration from occurring in both orientations. The resulting parallelogram gives two signed eigenvectors with disjoint supports and positive parts of sizes at least seven whose sum is at most 21. These are necessary conditions for the open existence problem.",
